---
description: Parsing of one test plain file into its conformance and acceptance scenarios.
import:
  - plain_parser_base
requires:
  - requires_chain
---

***definitions***

- :TestFileParser: is the `plain_parser.test_plain_file` module of :PythonApi:.

- :ScenarioRecord: is the parsed form of one :Scenario:: its :ScenarioId:, the line number of its heading, its text, its links and its code variables.

- :ParsedTestFile: is the parsed form of one :TestPlainFile:: the name it is shown by in messages, the module and func-id it binds to, and the :ScenarioRecord: lists of its conformance and acceptance sections.

***implementation reqs***

- :TestFileParser: reads scenarios line by line from the rendered text, not with a markdown parser; only links are found by parsing the scenario text with mistletoe.

***test reqs***

- :ConformanceTests: of code fences build each file as `---`, `module: todo`, `func_id: add-task`, `---`, an empty line, `***conformance tests***`, an empty line, the scenario, an empty line and `***acceptance tests***`, named `add-task.plain`.

- :ConformanceTests: of invalid test plain files start each file with the frontmatter `module: task-manager` and `func_id: add-task` on lines 1 to 4 and an empty line 5, and name it as the scenario's expected message does.

***functional specs***

# recognize-test-plain-file
:TestFileParser: tells whether a :Frontmatter: mapping makes its file a :TestPlainFile:: it does when it has both keys `module` and `func_id`.

# read-test-frontmatter
:TestFileParser: reads the :Frontmatter: of a :TestPlainFile: text, given with the name it is shown by.
- Invalid YAML raises `Test plain file '{name}' has invalid frontmatter: {error}`.
- A missing `module` or `func_id` key raises `has no `{key}:` key in its frontmatter`, `module` checked first.
- A value that is not a non-empty string raises `the frontmatter key `{key}:` must be a non-empty string`.
- Any other key raises `has the frontmatter key `{key}:`; only `module:` and `func_id:` are allowed`.
- All messages are the `PlainSyntaxError` texts of :ErrorMessages:.

# read-scenarios
:TestFileParser: parses a :TestPlainFile: text into a :ParsedTestFile: whose scenarios are in file order.
- Line endings are normalized and Liquid is rendered as for a :Module:, over :TemplateDirs:, before the lines are read; line numbers count from the first line of the file.
- A line that starts with three asterisks is a section line; the sections are `conformance tests` then `acceptance tests`, both required, either may be empty, and they may be on adjacent lines.
- A line that starts with `# `, or is only `#`, is a scenario heading; the rest of the line, stripped, is the :ScenarioId:.
- Every :CommentLine: is dropped, before the first section too.
- The scenario text is every other line up to the next heading or section line, as written, without leading and trailing blank lines.
- A :ScenarioRecord: as a mapping is `{"id", "text", "linked_resources"}`, plus `code_variables` when it has any.

# reject-invalid-scenarios
:TestFileParser: rejects an invalid :TestPlainFile: with the `PlainSyntaxError` texts of :ErrorMessages:, naming the line and the file.
- An unknown section line, a repeated section, and `acceptance tests` before `conformance tests` are rejected.
- A missing section is rejected after the whole file is read.
- A scenario heading or another non-blank line before the first section is rejected.
- A non-blank line in a section before its first heading is rejected.
- A heading whose id is not a :ScenarioId: is rejected; so is an id used twice in one file, naming the line of the first use.
- A scenario without text is rejected at its heading line.

# keep-code-fences
:TestFileParser: keeps every line inside a fenced code block as scenario text.
- A fence opens at a line whose text, after leading spaces, starts with three backticks or three tildes, and closes at the next such line with the same marker; a line with the other marker inside is text.
- Inside a fence, a `# ` line is not a heading, a three-asterisk line is not a section line, and a `>` line is not a comment.
- A fence still open when its scenario ends raises `Scenario `{id}` has a code fence that is not closed.` at the heading line.
- A fence before the first heading of a section is content before a heading.

# resolve-scenario-links
:TestFileParser: records the links of each scenario text as its `linked_resources`, resolved over :TemplateDirs: with the checks of a :Module: link.
- Error messages add ` in test plain file '{name}', scenario `{id}`` after the target, or after `Link` and before `must have text`.
- Code-variable tokens in a scenario text are replaced with placeholders and recorded in the scenario's `code_variables`, as for a :Module:.

# check-scenario-concepts
:TestFileParser: checks the concepts a :ParsedTestFile: mentions against a given set of visible concepts.
- It returns one error per scenario and distinct concept that is neither visible nor one of :PredefinedConcepts:, in scenario order, as `Syntax error at line {line} in test plain file '{name}': Concept {concept} used in scenario `{id}` is not defined in module '{module}'.`, the line being the heading line.
- It returns an empty list when every concept is visible.
